Fork notes (this entry, markdown-render-speed-02)
-------------------------------------------------
In THIS vendored copy, marked.esm.js carries a performance fork. No rendering
rule changes; every change only skips work whose outcome is already decided,
so the output is byte-for-byte identical (verified 720/720 against the
competition practice set):

1. Block lexer dispatch: in the block tokenization loop, each block
   tokenizer is attempted only when the block's first characters can still
   match it (for example, headings only when the first non-space character
   is '#', fences only for a backtick or tilde, HTML only for '<'). Every
   guard is a conservative superset of the rule it guards — it may pass
   when the rule would fail, never fail when the rule would pass — and the
   original attempt order is preserved, so the first matching tokenizer is
   always the same one pristine marked would have found.
2. Inline lexer dispatch + masking skips: inline tokenizers are dispatched
   per position by the same conservative first-character test (escape only
   for a backslash, codespan only for a backtick, and so on). In the
   emphasis masking step, the punctuation-masking pass is skipped when the
   source contains no backtick and no backslash (the pass's pattern can
   only match those), and the block-skip pass is skipped when the source
   contains none of '[', backtick or '<' (its alternatives each require
   one of those). A skipped pass is one that provably could not rewrite
   anything.
3. List tokenizer line scanning: the list tokenizer takes first lines with
   indexOf('\n') + slice instead of split('\n', 1)[0] — the same string,
   without allocating the split array — and the tab-expansion helper
   returns its input unchanged when the input contains no tab, which is
   exactly what the expansion loop would produce character by character.
